<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-27">27 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Britain and France recognise Franco's government</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> fallen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> recognised the Nationalist regime as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s government a month before the Civil War ended. Franco ruled Spain until his death in 1975.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-1">1 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Franco declares the Spanish Civil War over</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">With the last Republican forces surrendered, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a> announced the end of the war. His victory began a dictatorship that would last until his death in 1975.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-7">7 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Italy invades Albania</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s forces landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a> and other ports, and within days <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zog-i">King Zog</a> had fled. Albania was joined to the Italian crown, and the invasion later served as a base for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-steel" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pact-of-steel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-22">22 May</a> · Treaty</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-steel">Pact of Steel</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Pact of Steel was a military and political alliance between Fascist Italy and Nazi Germany, signed in Berlin on 22 May 1939 by the foreign ministers Galeazzo Ciano and Joachim von Ribbentrop.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/moscow" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moscow.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-23">23 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union sign a non-aggression pact</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Ribbentrop and Molotov signed a pact in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> whose secret protocol divided eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> into spheres of influence. It cleared the way for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> a week later.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-27">27 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Heinkel He 178 makes the first jet-powered flight</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Test pilot Erich Warsitz flew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinkel">Heinkel</a> He 178 at Rostock-Marienehe, the first flight of an aircraft powered by a turbojet. Days later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-31">31 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">SS men stage a fake Polish attack at Gleiwitz</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">SS operatives seized the German radio station at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gliwice">Gleiwitz</a> and broadcast an anti-German message, posing as Polish raiders. Hitler used such incidents to justify invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> the next morning.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-world-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-1">1 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Germany invades Poland</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">German forces crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> at dawn without a declaration of war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> declared war two days later, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> had begun.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-3">3 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Britain and France declare war on Germany</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> ignored ultimatums to withdraw from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> declared war. Neville Chamberlain announced it to the nation in a sombre radio broadcast.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-world-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-10">10 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Canada declares war on Germany</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A week after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>'s parliament declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in its own right. More than a million Canadians served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-23">23 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Sigmund Freud dies in London</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Freud</a>, who had fled Nazi-occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> the year before, died aged 83 after his doctor gave him lethal doses of morphine at his request. His theories of the unconscious reshaped twentieth-century thought.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Sigmund Freud</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-11">11 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Roosevelt receives Einstein's warning about atomic bombs</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The economist Alexander Sachs delivered to Franklin Roosevelt a letter signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a>, warning that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> might build a uranium bomb. Roosevelt set up an advisory committee that led eventually to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan-project">Manhattan Project</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winter-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-26">26 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Red Army shells Mainila to create a pretext for war</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> shelled the Soviet border village of Mainila near Beloostrov, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> claimed that the fire came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>. The false flag operation gave it a casus belli for launching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> four days later.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-30">30 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Soviet Union invades Finland</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Soviet forces attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, starting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a>. Fierce Finnish resistance inflicted heavy losses before Finland ceded territory in March 1940, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>'s poor showing was noted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-13">13 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">British cruisers fight the Graf Spee at the River Plate</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Three British cruisers engaged the German pocket battleship Admiral Graf Spee off the River Plate estuary. Damaged, she took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>, and four days later her captain scuttled her.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/theatre" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theatre.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-15">15 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Gone with the Wind premieres in Atlanta</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The film of Margaret Mitchell's novel premiered at Loew's Grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> in Atlanta. It won eight competitive Academy Awards, and allowing for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> it remains the highest-grossing film ever.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-17">17 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Graf Spee is scuttled off Montevideo</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Believing a superior British force waited outside, Captain Hans Langsdorff scuttled the Admiral Graf Spee in the River Plate. He took his own life three days later.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact">Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact was the non-aggression treaty signed by Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union in Moscow on 23 August 1939.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/battles-of-khalkhin-gol" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battles-of-khalkhin-gol.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battles-of-khalkhin-gol">Battles of Khalkhin Gol</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battles of Khalkhin Gol, known in Japan as the Nomonhan Incident, were fought from May to September 1939 on the Khalkhin Gol river on the border between Mongolia and Japanese-controlled Manchukuo.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/cvetkovic-macek-agreement">Cvetković–Maček Agreement</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Cvetković–Maček Agreement was a political settlement reached on 26 August 1939 between the Yugoslav prime minister Dragiša Cvetković and Vladko Maček, leader of the Croatian Peasant Party, with the backing of the regent Prince Paul.</p></div></li></ol></section>
